{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_011CdVkDbey5ZBfvjnhCgqB1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user is asking about the `p9` skill - specifically what happens if they close their laptop while a `p9 wait-for` or similar productive-wait operation is running. They're worried about background jobs that silently die, and they want to know:\n\n1. What happens if the laptop shuts down while `p9` is waiting\n2. What picks it back up\n3. Whether the wait \"evaporates\" or persists\n\nThis is a legitimate concern about reliability and observability. Let me think about what I actually know about p9:\n\nFrom the skill description:\n- \"p9: P9 — Broomva productive-wait primitive (the wait optimizer). Convert any blocking external operation — PR CI checks, push-triggered deploys, builds, long-running index ops — into work on the next priority.\"\n- It mentions \"the termination invariant (BRO-1701): on success, failure, OR kill it reports state + next action and pushes through the configured notify channels — killed watchers never die silently\"\n- It's described as a \"Broomva productive-wait primitive\"\n\nBut honestly, the description doesn't fully explain the infrastructure behind it. Is it:\n- A local process that dies when the laptop sleeps?\n- A background daemon?\n- A cloud service?\n- Something that persists state?\n\nThe description says \"killed watchers never die silently\" and mentions \"pushes through the configured notify channels\" - which suggests there's some notification/reporting mechanism. But it doesn't clarify whether the wait is local or remote.\n\nThe user is right to be cautious - they've been burned before and want to know what they're relying on.\n\nI should be honest: the skill description doesn't fully specify the infrastructure/durability guarantees. I can tell them:\n1. What the documentation claims (termination invariant, never dies silently)\n2. That I should actually read the p9 documentation or help them verify this before they rely on it\n3. They should ask or test this before putting critical work on it\n4. Their skepticism is warranted given the stakes - this deserves a concrete answer, not speculation about what might happen under the hood.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":10,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6935,"cache_read_input_tokens":17536,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6935},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"REDACTED","uuid":"196e68c1-733d-463a-96b9-ddf056623403","timestamp":"2026-07-29T04:50:00.594Z","request_id":"req_011CdVkDadhfV7EoPAfnmjC6"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_011CdVkDbey5ZBfvjnhCgqB1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Good question—you're right to ask before relying on it. Honestly, the skill description doesn't fully specify the infrastructure. It says the termination invariant guarantees \"on success, failure, OR kill it reports state + next action and pushes through the configured notify channels — killed watchers never die silently,\" which *sounds* solid. But that doesn't tell you whether it's:\n\n- A local process that dies when your laptop sleeps (bad)\n- A background daemon with persistence (better)\n- A cloud service that outlives your machine (best for long waits)\n\nThe description calls it a \"Broomva productive-wait primitive,\" which suggests it's part of some larger system, but I can't tell from the docs what the durability model actually is.\n\n**Before you ship critical work on this:** ask whoever maintains it or test it yourself—close the laptop mid-wait and see if the notification still lands, or if there's a way to resume/check state later. The \"never dies silently\" claim is good, but you need to know *what form* the notification takes and whether you'll actually see it if you're offline.\n\nIf it turns out to be local-process-only, that's useful to know too—you'd just use it for waits you can keep the machine awake for, and use something else (or manual checks) for anything that needs to survive a shutdown.\n\nWant me to help you test the actual behavior, or would you rather just stick with simpler polling for now?"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":10,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6935,"cache_read_input_tokens":17536,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6935},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_